AF-EP-005 — Architectural Continuity Before Extension

Parent Architecture: ADR-003 — Semantic Runtime Architecture

Purpose

To ensure that enhancements to the Semantic Runtime Architecture preserve constitutional continuity by extending established workflows before introducing new processing stages, services, or responsibilities.

Principle

Before implementing any enhancement within the Semantic Runtime Architecture, the existing workflow shall be reaffirmed to determine whether the proposed capability naturally extends an existing architectural responsibility.

Architectural evolution shall occur through extension of constitutional workflows, not through creation of parallel processing paths.

Relationship to ADR-003

This principle governs the evolution of the Semantic Runtime Architecture defined by ADR-003.

It does not alter the architecture established by ADR-003. Instead, it provides the constitutional decision framework used to evaluate future enhancements, ensuring that new capabilities strengthen existing architectural workflows rather than fragmenting them.

Principle

Implementation shall first reaffirm the existing architecture before extending it.

New capabilities shall be evaluated against established workflows to determine whether they naturally extend existing architectural responsibilities or require the creation of genuinely new architectural constructs.

Architectural continuity shall always take precedence over architectural expansion.

Architectural Rationale

Large software systems naturally evolve over time through incremental enhancements. Without deliberate architectural discipline, these enhancements often introduce redundant processing, overlapping responsibilities, duplicated logic, and fragmented workflows.

Such architectural drift increases technical debt, reduces maintainability, weakens governance, and makes future evolution increasingly difficult.

HCGO adopts a constitutional approach to architecture in which every enhancement first confirms its relationship to the existing architecture before introducing additional components.

The objective is not to minimize change, but to ensure change occurs as an intentional extension of the established constitutional architecture.

Decision Framework

Before introducing any new architectural capability, the following questions shall be answered:

Does an existing architectural workflow already own this responsibility?
Does an existing architectural component already provide this capability?
Can the proposed enhancement extend an existing workflow without violating constitutional responsibilities?
Would introducing a new service create duplicate ownership or overlapping responsibilities?
Does the enhancement preserve the existing authority model?

Only after these questions have been evaluated shall new architectural components be introduced.

Constitutional Rules

Every architectural enhancement shall:

• Reaffirm the existing workflow before modification.

• Extend existing constitutional responsibilities whenever appropriate.

• Preserve single ownership of architectural responsibilities.

• Avoid duplicate semantic processing.

• Maintain clear orchestration boundaries.

• Preserve Separation of Concerns.

• Preserve Single Responsibility.

• Preserve constitutional authority relationships.

Authority Model

Architectural continuity is governed by existing constitutional ownership.

Examples include:

QueryService remains the sole orchestration authority.
Semantic Runtime Pipeline remains the sole authority for semantic understanding.
Knowledge Retrieval remains the authority for authoritative knowledge acquisition.
Presentation components remain responsible only for rendering.

New capabilities shall extend these authorities rather than creating parallel ownership.

