Intent Reaffirmation extends the established Semantic Runtime Pipeline rather than creating a new architectural path.

Benefits

Applying this principle:

Preserves architectural integrity.
Reduces architectural drift.
Prevents duplicate responsibilities.
Simplifies debugging and troubleshooting.
Improves long-term maintainability.
Strengthens constitutional governance.
Enables predictable architectural evolution.
Reduces technical debt.
Relationship to Other Architectural Principles

AF-EP-005 reinforces, but does not replace:

Separation of Concerns
Single Responsibility Principle
Constitutional Authority Model
QueryService as Orchestration Authority
Semantic Runtime Authority
Implementation Consumes Architecture

Together these principles ensure HCGO evolves through disciplined architectural extension rather than incremental architectural fragmentation.

Architectural Lesson Learned (Version 5.6)

During implementation of the Version 5.6 Intent Reaffirmation capability, the initial instinct was to identify a location for a new processing step. Before implementation proceeded, the existing Semantic Runtime Pipeline was reviewed. That review confirmed that Intent Classification was already an established constitutional stage within the pipeline.

Rather than introducing a parallel semantic workflow, Intent Reaffirmation was positioned as the next semantic processing stage consuming the authoritative output of Intent Classification.

This review reaffirmed an important constitutional practice:

Every significant enhancement should begin by reaffirming the existing architectural workflow before introducing new capabilities.

By first understanding the current architecture, HCGO preserved constitutional continuity, avoided duplicate responsibilities, and strengthened the existing Semantic Runtime Pipeline instead of fragmenting it.
